</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results reveal the following key insights: (1) GADL consistently achieves the highest alignment accuracy across both real-world datasets, outperforming all baselines. In particular, it surpasses the second-best models—T-GAE on ACM-DBLP and SLOTAlign on Douban—by substantial margins, demonstrating its robustness in unsupervised real-world graph alignment scenarios. (2) Compared to T-GAE, which employs a GAE framework with a GIN encoder but lacks latent-space communication, our GADL model demonstrates superior performance through its dual-pass GCN encoder architecture integrated with latent-space communication. This design enables GADL to generate superior node embeddings, improving performance on unsupervised graph alignment tasks. (3) Beyond GADL, other learning-based methods (e.g., T-GAE) also outperform optimal-transport approaches (e.g., SLOTAlign) on larger benchmarks (e.g., ACM-DBLP), demonstrating superior robustness of learning-based methods to structural variations in large graphs that violate optimal transport assumptions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Generalization: evaluation on vision–language benchmarks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Latent space alignment is a special case of graph alignment, relying only on embeddings without explicit structure. To highlight this generality, we further evaluate our method on vision-language alignment benchmarks, where the task involves aligning latent representations from diverse pretrained vision and language models.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We evaluate latent space alignment across multiple benchmarks using representations from diverse pretrained vision and language models. Full experimental details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS4" title="C.4 Details of vision–language experiment ‣ Appendix C Experimental setup ‣ Graph Alignment via Dual-Pass Spectral Encoding and Latent Space Communication" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Generalization: evaluation on vision–language benchmarks ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Graph Alignment via Dual-Pass Spectral Encoding and Latent Space Communication" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> summarizes the vision-language alignment accuracies on four datasets using three pretrained vision models (CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, DeiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oquab et al., 2023</a>)</cite>) and two pretrained language models from SentenceTransformers library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimers and Gurevych, 2019</a>)</cite> (all-mpnet-base-v2 and all-roberta-large-v1). Comprehensive results for all vision and language model combinations are provided in the Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Comprehensive results for vision-language model combinations ‣ Graph Alignment via Dual-Pass Spectral Encoding and Latent Space Communication" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.
Results on CIFAR-100 and ImageNet-100 are reproduced using official implementations.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Vision-language alignment across four datasets using three pretrained vision models (CLIP, DeiT, and DINOv2) and two pretrained language models: Lan. model 1 (all-mpnet-base-v2) and Lan. model 2 (all-roberta-large-v1).</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:171.7pt;vertical-align:-84.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-144.5pt,71.9pt) scale(0.544174947593794,0.544174947593794) ;">
<table id="S4.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"></td>
