<td id="S4.T3.2.1.26.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.26.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">58.3 ± 0.4</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.26.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.26.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">44.3 ± 8.3</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.26.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.26.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">49.3 ± 0.4</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The experimental results highlight several key insights.
(1) GADL consistently outperforms all baselines across datasets and pretrained models, demonstrating that moving beyond optimization- and optimal transport–based frameworks provides substantial benefits.
(2) Most baselines achieve near-chance accuracies (<math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 10\%</annotation></semantics></math>) with occasional inconsistent successes, and even sophisticated solvers like Gurobi or Hahn-Grant fail in certain settings. This inconsistency intensifies with dataset complexity, and the performance gap with GADL becomes pronounced on challenging benchmarks (CIFAR-100, ImageNet-100), underscoring the limitations of treating vision-language alignment as pure assignment optimization.
(3) The choice of pretrained model critically impacts cross-modal alignment performance. While DINOv2 and DeiT achieve highest accuracies on smaller datasets, CLIP consistently outperforms on larger, more complex benchmarks. This highlights how pretrained model selection significantly influences visual-language alignment effectiveness.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ablation analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To better understand the contribution of each component in the proposed GADL framework, we conduct an ablation study evaluating variants with specific modules removed or modified. We compare GADL against: (1) <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GADL w/o dual-pass encoder</span>: replaces the dual-pass GCN with a standard single-pass GCN while retaining latent-space communication; (2) <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GADL w/o bijectivity regularization</span>: removes the bijectivity regularization term while keeping the dual-pass encoder; (3) <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GADL w/o orthogonality regularization</span>: omits orthogonality regularization while maintaining all other components.</p>
</div>
